Asthma (Exercise/Environmental Triggers)
Respiratory System
Episodic wheezing, Reversible bronchospasm, Atopic history

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-008
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute episodic wheezing and shortness of breath following environmental exposure, requiring urgent pharmacological intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young patient experiencing severe exertional dyspnea and accessory muscle use after playing in cold air. Urgent assessment was required to reverse acute bronchospasm and prevent progression to status asthmaticus prior to the regular in-hours clinic opening.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I was running outside in the cold and suddenly my chest locked up and I can't stop coughing or wheezing."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 45 minutes ago during physical exertion in cold weather. The patient describes transient chest tightness and an acute onset of episodic wheezing shortly after exposure. There is a prominent dry cough that the patient notes is also frequent early in the morning. The exertional dyspnea has only partially resolved with rest. The patient has a known personal atopic history of allergic rhinitis and eczema. Symptoms typically resolve completely between episodes.
Functional Status: Independent, currently resting upright but breathless while talking.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety; suboptimal inhaler technique reported by parent.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 26 breaths/min | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 45 Kg
General: Alert, anxious, utilizing intermittent accessory muscles during respiration, audible wheeze from the end of the bed.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals a prolonged expiratory phase with widespread polyphonic wheeze throughout all lung fields. Air entry is fair but reduced bilaterally. Skin exam shows active flexural eczema. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates a rapid, regular pulse consistent with beta-agonist use and anxiety.
Environmental Observations: Cat present in the household; open windows exposing the room to cold draft.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol MDI used 6 times in the last hour without a spacer device.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Asthma (Exercise/Environmental Triggers) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of urticaria, angioedema, hypotension, or gastrointestinal symptoms.

Vocal Cord Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of expiratory polyphonic wheeze rather than localized inspiratory stridor in the neck.

Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical wheeze rather than unilateral decreased air entry, and clear history of environmental trigger.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting with acute, reversible bronchospasm triggered by cold air and exertion, on a background of a strong atopic history. The presence of tachycardia, tachypnea, and intermittent accessory muscle use indicates moderate acute asthma severity. The patient's excessive reliance on an MDI without a spacer has resulted in inadequate drug delivery to the lower airways. Deferring this assessment until the regular in-hours period would pose an unacceptable risk of impending respiratory exhaustion. An urgent home visit was essential to administer high-dose nebulized bronchodilators, correct inhaler technique, and establish immediate stability.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Salbutamol 5mg via nebulizer resulting in marked clinical improvement and reduction in work of breathing. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 40mg daily for 3 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: Attach the inhaler to a spacer device and take one slow, deep breath, holding it for 10 seconds; this precise action guarantees the medication deposits in the small airways rather than the tongue, resulting in immediate relief of chest tightness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a new large-volume spacer.
Follow-up: GP review within 48 hours for spirometry to document bronchodilator reversibility and upgrade maintenance preventer therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 if there is no relief from the reliever inhaler after 10 puffs, if the patient struggles to speak in sentences, or if the ribs pull in sharply with breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of acute exacerbation is clinical; formal spirometry when well is required to confirm diagnostic criteria.
Clinical Course: The acute bronchospasm has been reversed, but the airways remain hyper-reactive. The oral steroids will prevent a secondary inflammatory rebound over the next 72 hours.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care immediately if the wheezing returns rapidly, if your chest feels completely silent, or if you feel too exhausted to breathe.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cold air caused the muscles around your breathing tubes to spasm; the nebulizer relaxed the muscles, and the steroid tablets will remove the underlying swelling.
Actionable Steps: Take your preventer inhaler every single day, even when you feel perfectly well; establishing this daily habit builds a protective shield in your lungs, directly preventing cold air from triggering future spasms.

